    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d91">

                <pb facs="0332" n="316" xml:id="pg316"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.00-fa/5-1551</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Representative of the Department of
                        Defense on the Executive Group on Foreign Aid</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Lincoln</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Under Secretary of State</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Webb</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1951-05-15">May 15, 1951</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Here are the “Essential Points of Mutual Security Program”, which I promised I
                    would attempt to draft.</p>
                <p>You suggested that some outline such as the attached might be the basis for
                    discussion with Mr. Richards<anchor n="1" xml:id="d91-f1"/> this afternoon with
                    the thought that we might want to learn more about the best method of approach
                    to Senator George<anchor n="2" xml:id="d91-f2"/> and other members of
                    Congress.</p>


                <xi:include href="d91-a1.xml" xpointer="d91-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d91-f1" n="1" xml:id="d91-f1-content">
                <p>Representative James P. Richards of South Carolina became Chairman of the House
                    Committee on Foreign Affairs on May 15, succeeding John Kee of West Virginia who
                    died on May 8.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d91-f2" n="2" xml:id="d91-f2-content">
                <p>Senator Walter F. George of Georgia, Member of the Senate Foreign Relations
                    Committee.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
